A hormone is a polypeptide of 85 amino acids, counting the first methionine. Calculate:

(a) the minimum number of nucleotides in the part of the mRNA that codes for the amino acids;

(b) the number of nucleotides in the mRNA from the start codon to the end of the stop codon;

(c) the number of peptide bonds in the polypeptide;

(d) the number of base pairs in the double-stranded DNA of the gene between the start and stop codons, assuming the gene has no introns.
